As with any large and flexible application, things are complicated The place to start looking at is /login/index.php. This is where all the log in machinery is started. Depending on a lot of configuration settings (SSO, login page hooks, etc.) the login flow can vary wildly. To simplify things, I'm going to assume that Emma is not using CAS, Shibboleth , OAuth2, LTI or Web services authentication, or anything like that. I.E. only LDAP plus good old manual authentication. I'm also assuming that no "Alternative login URL" has been configured, and that Emma might be using NTLM_SSO. In that case, things start at line 73 (in Moodle 3.4.1+ (Build: 20180308)), where we get the list of enabled authenticated plugins, apparently in the order the admin configured them. We can check for that including a debugging statement there, dumping the content of $authsequence: 
```text
$authsequence = get_enabled_auth_plugins(true); // auths, in sequence
debugging('$authsequece: ' . print_r($authsequence, true)); // +++ Add this line +++
foreach($authsequence as $authname) {
    $authplugin = get_auth_plugin($authname);
    $authplugin->loginpage_hook();
}
```
 LDAP auth plugins implement NTLM_SSO inside the loginpage_hook() method, so if any of Emma's LDAP plugins has NTLM_SSO enabled, they will "stop" the normal login flow. I.e., the rest of the code in login/index.php won't be executed, not even the next iteration of the loop. If the LDAP plugins don't have NTLM_SSO enabled (and we assumed there's no alternative login url configured), and the user is not already logged in and hasn't submitted the username and password yet, the good old login form is presented, so the user can type her username and password. When the form is submitted, it starts from the top of /login/index.php again. The first few things work exactly as before (login page hook et al.), but this time we have a username and password that have been submitted. We call authenticate_user_login (in lib/moodlelib.php) and pass in the username and the password. The first thing we do there is checking whether the user already exists or not, and do different things depending on it. Skipping a lot of checks, we get to the part where new (non-existing) users are tested against the list of enabled auth plugins. I see the way we get that list is slightly different that the way we do it in login/index.php, but it shouldn't matter most of the time[1]. So around line 4292 in lib/moodelib.php we see this code: 
```text
foreach ($auths as $auth) {
    $authplugin = get_auth_plugin($auth);

    // On auth fail fall through to the next plugin.
    if (!$authplugin->user_login($username, $password)) {
        continue;
    }

    // Successful authentication.
    ...
```
 where $auths is the list of enabled plugins, apparently ordered as the administrator configured them. Again, we can add another debugging statement just before the foreach() line, to dump it to the debugging output, We can even add a second debugging statement to record what specific authentication plugins are tried: 
```text
debugging('$auths: ' . print_r($auths, true)); // ++ Add this line ++
foreach ($auths as $auth) {
    $authplugin = get_auth_plugin($auth);
    debugging('$authplugin: ' . print_r($authplugin, true)); // ++ Add this line ++
    ...
```
 That way, if the login is successful, we know that the last printed value for $authplugin is the name of the plugin that validated the user, and thus the authentication plugin that will be used in the future for that user (unless manually changed by an administrator). There are many additional details that are checked and can influence the final result, but with the assumptions made above, this is roughly how it should work. So Emma, if you can add the debugging statements and try with one new (problematic) user, we might have a clue about where things are turning wrong.[2] Saludos. Iñaki. [1] Inside authenticate_user_login we don't remove authentication plugins that are no longer installed, or remove duplicate plugin names. But that shouldn't create the behavior Emma is experiencing. [2] It might very well be a bug in my patches

I don't think this is possible without modifying/extending some core code. The password policy checking function doesn't have an extension point built in. And the authentication plugins are not assumed to check the password policy themselves. Moodle assumes that you check your password policy centrally, and if the password passes the check, then the auth plugins simply perform the actions to update it wherever they have to. So I'd say that the less invasive way to achieve what you want is to add a config setting (either in config.php or directly in the database , in prefix_settings table) with the name of a function that would perform as many additional policy checks as you want. Let's assume for the sake of the example that the setting is called $CFG->additional_password_policy_checks_function. The function would need to take the same two parameters as check_password_policy() function: a string with the new password to check an error message string, passed in by reference, where you would add (concatenate) your additional error messages. Then you would just add something like this near the end of check_password_policy() 
```text
if (!check_consecutive_identical_characters($password, $CFG->maxconsecutiveidentchars)) {
    $errmsg .= '<div>'. get_string('errormaxconsecutiveidentchars', 'auth', $CFG->maxconsecutiveidentchars) .'</div>';
}

// -- Add from this line --
if(!empty($CFG->additional_password_policy_checks_function)) {
    call_user_func_array($CFG->additional_password_policy_checks_function,
                         array($password, &$errmsg));
}
// -- Add to this line --

if ($errmsg == '') {
    return true;
} else {
    return false;
}
```
 That seems to work as expected in my test Moodle site. Saludos. Iñaki.

