Hi Dave, So, is there any way to find out, without having access to the server or LDAP list, whether the account I'm creating manually actually matches the LDAP username credential, so I don't waste so much time when it turns out to be wrong? as Emma has told you, you can't do this from the web interface of Moodle, but if you have access to the command line in the server Moodle runs on, you can use the following PHP code to test whether a given username and password are valid or not: 
```text
<?php 

define('CLI_SCRIPT', true);
require(dirname(__FILE__).'/config.php');

print "Enter username to validate: ";
$username = trim(fgets(STDIN));
print "Enter password for username: ";
$password = trim(fgets(STDIN));

$ldap_auth = get_auth_plugin('ldap');

if (!$ldap_auth->user_login($username, $password)) {
    die ("\n\nUsername or password are incorrect!\n");
} else {
    echo "\n\nUsername and password seem to be correct.\n";
}
```
 You have to put the code in a file (say, test-ldap-user.php) and put that file in your Moodle top level directory (the same where config.php is). Then you can execute it from the command line with: 
```text
php test-ldap-user.php
```
 it will ask for a username and password and will try to validate them to your LDAP server using the LDAP settings you have configured in Moodle. Hope that helps. Saludos. Iñaki.

