While searching for a PHP function to shuffle a string to make generate_password use the password complexity options, I've found this nice piece of code http://es2.php.net/manual/en/function.str-shuffle.php#73254 , which can be extended to create a new generate_password function like this: 
```text
    define ('PASSWORD_LOWER', 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz');
    define ('PASSWORD_UPPER', 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ');
    define ('PASSWORD_DIGITS', '0123456789');
    DEFINE ('PASSWORD_NONALPHANUM', '.,;:!?_-+/*@#&$');
    //
    function generate_password ($length=10, $lower=1, $upper=1, $digits=1, $nonalphanum=1) {
        $additional = $length - ($lower + $upper + $digits + $nonalphanum);
        srand((double) microtime() * 1000000);
        $password = str_shuffle (substr(str_shuffle (PASSWORD_LOWER), 0, $lower) .
                                 substr(str_shuffle (PASSWORD_UPPER), 0, $upper) .
                                 substr(str_shuffle (PASSWORD_DIGITS), 0, $digits) .
                                 substr(str_shuffle (PASSWORD_NONALPHANUM), 0 , $nonalphanum) .
                                 substr(str_shuffle (PASSWORD_LOWER .
                                                     PASSWORD_UPPER .
                                                     PASSWORD_DIGITS .
                                                     PASSWORD_NONALPHANUM), 0 , $additional));
        return substr ($password, 0, $length);
    }
```
 We could add/remove more non alphanumeric characters, this is just a sample set. Much the same refering the lowercase/uppercase set: they are just a small subset of the UTF-8 [::alpha::] character set, but I'd say that's a safe bet (I don't want a poor US student trying to type the 'Ñ' symbol with a US keyboard ) Saludos. Iñaki.

